    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32780v1</id>
    <title>AI Alignment for Digital Democracy: Fairer Voters Representation against LLM Biases</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T15:27:40Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32780v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32780v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>AI representation is emerging in digital democracy, assisting in voter emulation and decision-making. Large language models (LLMs) behave differently depending on the model, the prompt context, and the voter information they receive, representing some groups more accurately than others and potentially making the emulation unfair to certain groups. We investigate demographic biases in LLM-emulated electorates: how accurately LLMs represent individual votes, whether some voter groups are represented more accurately than others, and whether AI alignment can reduce these disparities without sacrificing overall accuracy. Using three U.S. presidential elections, we compare a closed-source and an open-source LLM across voters' demographic features and test AI alignment strategies toward fairer voter representation. Removing gender from the voter profile reduces the largest group error of the models by 22-42%, and removing education reduces it by 64%, whereas removing self-reported race increases the mean group error by over 40%, all without lowering accuracy. Common alignment strategies, such as repeated queries or fairness instructions, do not reduce these errors and can double them.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CY" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-23T15:27:40Z</published>
