    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32361v1</id>
    <title>FOCUS: A Lightweight Mamba-2 Model for EEG-Based Cognitive Workload Classification</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T08:54:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32361v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32361v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Cognitive workload estimation from physiological signals is increasingly relevant in high-risk environments, where continuous and unobtrusive monitoring can inform adaptive systems and safety interventions. This paper presents FOCUS (Frequency-band Optimized Cognitive-load Ultralight SSM), a compact Mamba-2-based model for EEG-based binary workload classification under constrained data conditions. Rather than learning frequency sensitivity from data, FOCUS encodes preprocessed multi-channel EEG using a fixed FFT decomposition that covers the five canonical EEG bands. The proposed model combines depthwise-separable convolution and a weight-shared encoder-bottleneck Mamba-2 pair, with 0.04 million parameters and 0.11 GFLOPs. Evaluated in a pilot study with 11 participants under leave-one-subject-out cross-validation across three cognitively distinct tasks, FOCUS achieves 74.75% accuracy on the full dataset at 0.08 ms GPU model-forward latency. In the weight-sharing ablation, the shared configuration achieves 4.55 percentage points higher accuracy on the full dataset than the non-shared variant while reducing the SSM parameter budget; however, this difference does not reach statistical significance under a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test ($p=0.083$). Overall, the selected FOCUS configuration provides a favorable trade-off between classification performance and computational cost within this controlled pilot setting.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
