    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32333v1</id>
    <title>Beyond Final Outcomes: Quantifying Claim-Level Information Dynamics in Multi-Agent LLM Interaction</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T08:28:47Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32333v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32333v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>When LLM-powered agents interact, information is repeatedly shared, expanded, and forgotten, yet multi-agent evaluation typically focuses on final task outcomes. We study such interaction as claim-level information dynamics and propose RIVER, which decomposes agent outputs into atomic claims and evaluates three complementary dimensions: convergence, exploration, and retention. Convergence captures cross-agent claim correspondence and stance alignment; exploration captures semantic breadth and extension beyond the source; and retention tracks source preservation and the persistence of new claims across stages and lineages. We evaluate five interaction strategies on 40 controlled news-generation cases. Repeatedly pooled interaction promotes convergence but narrows the information space, whereas distributed relay yields broader, less shared information. Further comparisons show that method-specific constraints increase exploration while weakening convergence and retention. Our findings suggest that collective information dynamics are better explained by underlying information-flow mechanisms than by simple downstream performance.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-23T08:28:47Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>23 pages, 3 figures. Code and data: https://github.com/fzj-2023/RIVER</arxiv:comment>
